The routine lnx-a computes the natural logarithm of a whole number from 1 to 999, by pseudo-division, four-digit
remainder.

Input: x from 1 to 999. Result: 1000 ln x rounded, from 0 to 6908, in the accumulator (it does not fit a cell: STO
keeps the last three digits and SFT 03 leaves the thousands).

Call: load x into the accumulator and JMP 12. It returns with the result in the accumulator.
Cells 12-98 (87 cells). It writes cell 99 and, of its own cells, 65-68, 98; nothing else.
Cost: at most 284 instructions a call, 134.9 on average over the domain, counted from the calling JMP to the
return jump. Worst error: 1 in the last digit (thousandths) against the correctly rounded value, at x = 4; 110 of
the 999 inputs are off.
To move the routine down by d cells, subtract d from every address in its instructions that points into
cells 12-98; addresses 00 and 99 stay as they are. It holds no address bases.

